1. ON A LIST, += CHANGES THE OBJECT
     a = [1]
     b = a
     a += [2]
     b                                [1, 2]           b saw it: += grew the one list in place
     a is b                           True
     a = a + [3]
     b                                [1, 2]           + built a new list and bound a to it; b kept the old one
     a is b                           False

2. ON A TUPLE, A STRING OR AN INT, += REBINDS
     t = (1,)
     u = t
     t += (2,)
     u                                (1,)             a tuple cannot change, so t was rebound
     t is u                           False
     s = 'ab'
     r = s
     s += 'c'
     r                                'ab'
     n = 1
     m = n
     n += 1
     m                                1

3. THE METHOD THAT DECIDES
     hasattr(list, '__iadd__')        True             list can change itself, so += calls this
     hasattr(tuple, '__iadd__')       False            no __iadd__: += falls back to __add__, then assigns
     hasattr(str, '__iadd__')         False
     hasattr(int, '__iadd__')         False
     hasattr(bytearray, '__iadd__')   True             the same split as list against tuple
     hasattr(bytes, '__iadd__')       False
     a = [1]
     a.__iadd__([2]) is a             True             the in-place method returns the object it changed

4. THE TUPLE TRAP: IT RAISES, AND IT ALSO HAPPENED
     t = ([], 'x')
     t[0] += [1]                      TypeError
     t                                ([1], 'x')       the list grew, then the store back into the tuple failed
     t2 = ([], 'x')
     t2[0] = t2[0] + [1]              TypeError
     t2                               ([], 'x')        with +, nothing had happened when the store failed
     t3 = ([], 'x')
     t3[0].extend([1])
     t3                               ([1], 'x')       no store into the tuple, so no error

5. THROUGH AN INSTANCE, += ON A CLASS ATTRIBUTE
     class Counter: count = 0
     c = Counter()
     c.count += 1
     Counter.count                    0                the read found the class attribute
     vars(c)                          {'count': 1}     the store made an instance attribute
     class Log: lines = []
     lg = Log()
     lg.lines += ['a']
     Log.lines                        ['a']            a list: the read found the class's list and += grew it
     vars(lg)                         {'lines': ['a']} and the store still made an instance attribute
     lg.lines is Log.lines            True             which names the same, now shared, list

6. THE SAME SPLIT FOR *=
     a = [0]
     b = a
     a *= 2
     b                                [0, 0]           in place
     s = 'ab'
     r = s
     s *= 2
     r                                'ab'             rebound
